---
name: fix-task
version: 3.16.0
description: lead fix assignment in atm-bd-orchestration. task_id is a poured fix bead (`<sprint>.<ref>-r<n>-fix`, carrying its blocking finding) or an important or minor finding bead; the fix is a new layer at the top of the stack the finding was found on.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - stack
  - pr_target
  - worktree_path
  - branch
  - primary_checkout
  - phase_root
  - test_command
  - lint_command
  - policy_path
  - requirements_globs
  - adr_globs
metadata:
  type: fix-task
  tags:
    - stage:fix
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: fix-start
    stage: fix
    transition: start
---
<atm-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" mode="fix">
  <description><![CDATA[Fix `{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (found in sprint {{ sprint | string | cdata_escape }}). `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` is the assignment: a poured fix bead carries the finding in its description and `metadata.remedy`; a finding bead has the finding and evidence in its description, the remedy in its design, and when it is fixed in its acceptance criteria.]]></description>

  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>
  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <stack><![CDATA[{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}]]></stack>
  <pr-target><![CDATA[{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}]]></pr-target>
  <stack-discipline><![CDATA[The finding is on a frozen layer. `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` is a new layer cut from `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`, the top of stack `{{ stack | string | cdata_escape }}` at dispatch; its PR lands on the stack's current top. Work only on this branch. The task assigner is the stack writer: never run `gh stack link`, `sync`, `rebase` or `merge`.]]></stack-discipline>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Before claiming, in the worktree run `git fetch origin` (no rebase: the branch was cut from the stack top, and check (5), `--is-ancestor`, proves it holds `origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}`), then `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py dev --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }} --bead {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} --worktree {{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` (scripts come from the primary checkout, never the branch). Checks, commands and refusal codes: (1) plan valid: `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-beads/scripts/validate-plan --root {{ phase_root | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0, else `PLAN_INVALID`; (2) ready: `bd ready -n 0 --json` lists the bead, else `NOT_READY`; (3) claimable without `--force`: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --json` shows status `open` and assignee empty or `$ATM_IDENTITY`, else `UNCLAIMABLE`; (4) target: `metadata.pr_target` is `{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` or `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/<metadata.pr_target> origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` exits 0 (`pr_target` is a lower bound), else `PR_TARGET_MISMATCH`; (5) base: `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/{{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }} HEAD` exits 0, else `WRONG_BASE`; any command failing: `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`. On refusal, reuse an existing workflow class bead with the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite its id in the refusal; if none matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; never create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. On pass, claim with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --claim --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"`, then start `atm task start {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`.]]></step>
    <step id="a1"><![CDATA[When it prints `NOT_READY` the bead is not ready: do not claim it or start the task. Find the root cause: `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}` (status, assignee), `bd blocked --json | jq --arg b {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} '.[] | select(.id == $b)'` (open blockers: another fix that must land first), and `bd show <blocker>` for each. Then refuse; never wait: close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`, `bead_state` `open`, `reason_md` giving the bead, why it is not ready, which bead or agent must move, and, for a blocker not yet its dependency, the edge to add, `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`, so `bd ready` lists the bead only once the blocker closes. The task assigner then re-assigns it.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Read `bd show {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}`, then its `metadata.sprint_bead`, the sprint whose contract its requirements and ADRs come from, for the scope it belongs to. Read every id in the bead's own `metadata.requirements` and `metadata.adrs` (requirements: {{ requirements_globs | join(", ") | string | cdata_escape }}; ADRs: {{ adr_globs | join(", ") | string | cdata_escape }}); the fix must keep satisfying them. Before editing, read the repository QA policy `{{ policy_path | string | cdata_escape }}` and every guideline it names for the languages this fix touches.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Before fixing, verify the defect still exists at the cited file:line in this worktree. If it does not reproduce (already fixed, superseded, or code path gone), do no speculative or invented work for it: close it as not reproducible (step f), citing the file:line you checked.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Read the complete finding and remedy and list every required task; create an itemized private checklist outside the tracked tree with every task identified, and work through the checklist one item at a time. Fix every occurrence in scope, not just the first: for a repeatable pattern, search the worktree for it and fix each one. Touch only the files the remedy needs. Then go through the checklist again one item at a time, confirming each is done. Run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and `{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}` until they pass with zero failures; a lint failure inherited from a lower layer is yours to fix on this layer.]]></step>
    <step id="d1"><![CDATA[If the work needs a change other live branches need at the same time (a shared type or trait signature, or a critical bug every branch carries), stop that edit, tell the task assigner the exact change and the branches it breaks, follow the assigner's base (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md`, Parallel Quick Fix), and report a quick-fix PR you open by plain `atm send`.]]></step>
    <step id="e"><![CDATA[Rebase onto the stack's current top (it may have moved since dispatch): `git fetch origin`; the top is what `{{ primary_checkout | string | cdata_escape }}/.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/assignment-gates.py stack-top --pr-target {{ pr_target | string | cdata_escape }}` prints on exit 0 (from GitHub's stacks); any other exit prints a code (`STACK_AMBIGUOUS`, `GATE_CANNOT_RUN`): never guess, refuse with it by step f1. Then `git rebase origin/<top>`, re-run `{{ test_command | string | cdata_escape }}` and `{{ lint_command | string | cdata_escape }}`, and `git push --force-with-lease origin {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}`. Unless this quick-fix branch already has its open PR, open it against that top: `gh pr create --base <top> --head {{ branch | string | cdata_escape }} --fill` (never `--draft`); run `/sc-gh-stack-view` (read-only) and keep its output verbatim. Record the full head SHA, the top as `rebased_onto`, and the PR number and url.]]></step>
    <step id="f"><![CDATA[Close the bead and task together, `bd close` first; close the task only if `bd close` succeeded: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "fixed at <short sha>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` (or `--reason "not reproducible: <file:line checked>"`), then `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/fix-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/fix-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. Build the vars from that template's `required_variables`: `task_id` and `sprint` unchanged, `commit` the pushed head, `rebased_onto` the top, `pr_number` and `pr_url` from step e, `stack_view` the verbatim `/sc-gh-stack-view` output (omit these five for `not_reproducible`), the outcome, and the gate output lines; write the vars file outside the repository. The fix then gets its own sanity check and QA (the group's sanity and qa beads, which the lead created with this assignment for an important or minor finding; a quick-fix finding has no sanity check, only the quick fix's next QA bead). Only this close frees your queue.]]></step>
    <step id="f1"><![CDATA[If the fix cannot be done at all, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to the task assigner for classification and cite that message instead; never create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. Also announce a serious infrastructure failure once per cause to the oversight recipients (`.claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/SKILL.md` Lead Role). Leave the bead open with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "FIX_CANNOT_COMPLETE; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`. The fixer never closes a fix or finding bead unfixed; only quality-mgr (ceremony) or the task assigner does. A blocker is not a failure: if the task needs unfinished work another bead or agent owns (a prerequisite open or reopened, another agent's fix not landed), stop, return the bead with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "BLOCKED: <blocker>: <why>" --actor "$ATM_IDENTITY"` and close the task `refused` as above (`bead_state` `open`), its `reason_md` naming the blocker and the edge `bd dep add {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --blocked-by <blocker>`; never stay active waiting. A shared change you can make yourself is not a blocker: it is a Parallel Quick Fix (step d1) and the task continues.]]></step>
    <step id="g"><![CDATA[After the close, read ATM again: the next queued task starts only now.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-task>
